            <tr><td>WR-5</td><td>Chamfered</td><td>220 GHz</td><td>-41.82 dBsm</td></tr>
            <tr><td>WR-3</td><td>Blunt</td><td>330 GHz</td><td>-28.58 dBsm</td></tr>
            <tr><td>WR-3</td><td>Chamfered</td><td>330 GHz</td><td>-41.78 dBsm</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Three takeaways from that table, in the paper's own framing:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Boresight max RCS tracks the flat-plate tip-area formula closely enough to be a useful mental model.</li>
        <li>For a common wall and tip thickness family, RCS falls with rising frequency until an inflection set by the tip wall thickness. For a blunt 0.050 in wall that inflection is around WR-10. For a chamfered tip with 0.020 in tip wall it holds off until WR-3.</li>
        <li>The RCS reduction of chamfered versus blunt grows as frequency increases (equivalently, as the standard waveguide gets smaller). At WR-90 the gap is 0.64 dB. By WR-10 it is about 6 dB. By WR-3 it is more than 13 dB.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>That is the quantitative backing for the IEEE taper language: the chamfer is mainly a scattering control, and it matters more the higher you go.</p>

      <h2>What I would take into a range review</h2>
      <p>If you are buying, modeling, or arguing about OEWG probes on a planar near-field system:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Treat tip geometry as a scattering design choice first.</strong> At X-band, radiation pattern barely moves among the blunt, 10°, and 45° cases we ran. On-axis RCS is the term the chamfer mainly improves, and that improvement grows toward W-band and above.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not ignore tip wall thickness when you scale a probe family.</strong> A constant 0.050 in blunt wall hits an RCS floor near WR-10. Thinning the tip to 0.020 in with a chamfer pushes that floor out.</li>
        <li><strong>Validate the probe model the same way you validate the AUT model.</strong> Our blunt WR-90 radiation check against Yaghjian was the gate before any tip comparison. If the blunt reference is wrong, the chamfer differences are not meaningful.</li>
        <li><strong>Terminate the finite probe honestly in simulation.</strong> Exterior currents that reach the back edges, and interior port reflections, are not tip physics. We used a Taylor exterior resistive taper (or a bulk absorber in the second model) plus a resistive port sheet so those terms did not dominate the tip comparison.</li>
        <li><strong>Remember what was left out.</strong> The numerical exterior taper is not a stand-in for the physical absorber collar. Collar, mount, and the extreme near-field of a real AUT were outside this study. Tip RCS is necessary. It is not the whole multiple-reflection budget.</li>
        <li><strong>When sim and range disagree on a planar near-field job, put the probe on the suspect list.</strong> Tip sharpness, wall thickness, collar, and residual probe-AUT interaction are all fair game before you blame the transform.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Paper and next steps</h2>
      <p>The full write-up is E. H. Ko and D. J. Belgiovane, "Radiation and Scattering Pattern Characteristics of Chamfered-Tip Open-Ended Rectangular Waveguide Probes for Planar Near-Field Antenna Measurement Applications," Antenna Measurement Techniques Association Symposium (AMTA 2021). <a href="https://doi.org/10.23919/AMTA52830.2021.9620710">doi:10.23919/AMTA52830.2021.9620710</a>. We thanked Justin A. Dobbins and Robert G. Yaccarino for guidance, and Dr. Edwin Barry at NSI-MI Technologies for information on commercial planar near-field probes.</p>
      <p>Suggested follow-on work in the paper, still worth doing: intercardinal radiation and scattering cuts, and extreme-near-field AUT studies that quantify how much a chamfered tip actually reduces probe-AUT interaction on a real scanner.</p>
      <p>If you are fighting a planar near-field residual, picking a probe family, or trying to put honest error bars on probe-AUT interaction, I do paid one-hour design reviews. <a href="https://calendar.app.google/BkzA1rurMrozK5Ta9">Book a one-hour design review</a>.</p>
